{"st":"KY","name":"Kentucky","seats":[{"census":1790,"seats":2},{"census":1800,"seats":6},{"census":1810,"seats":10},{"census":1820,"seats":12},{"census":1830,"seats":13},{"census":1840,"seats":10},{"census":1850,"seats":10},{"census":1860,"seats":9},{"census":1870,"seats":10},{"census":1880,"seats":11},{"census":1890,"seats":11},{"census":1900,"seats":11},{"census":1910,"seats":11},{"census":1920,"seats":11},{"census":1930,"seats":9},{"census":1940,"seats":9},{"census":1950,"seats":8},{"census":1960,"seats":7},{"census":1970,"seats":7},{"census":1980,"seats":7},{"census":1990,"seats":6},{"census":2000,"seats":6},{"census":2010,"seats":6},{"census":2020,"seats":6}],"events":[{"congress":2,"year":1792,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Kentucky became the 15th state on June 1, 1792.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html"]},{"congress":8,"year":1802,"kind":"census","title":"1800 census apportionment","text":"After the 1800 census, Kentucky's House delegation grew from 2 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":13,"year":1812,"kind":"census","title":"1810 census apportionment","text":"After the 1810 census, Kentucky's House delegation grew from 6 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, Kentucky's House delegation grew from 10 to 12 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, Kentucky's House delegation grew from 12 to 13 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, Kentucky's House delegation shrank from 13 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Kentucky kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Kentucky's House delegation shrank from 10 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Kentucky's House delegation grew from 9 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Kentucky's House delegation grew from 10 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Kentucky kept 11 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Kentucky kept 11 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Kentucky kept 11 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Kentucky's House delegation shrank from 11 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 73rd Congress (1933–1935), Kentucky elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district. The 1929 apportionment law had no districting rules, so states without a new map after losing or gaining seats could elect members at-large.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/","https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IF12567.html"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Kentucky kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Kentucky's House delegation shrank from 9 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Kentucky's House delegation shrank from 8 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Kentucky kept 7 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Kentucky kept 7 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Kentucky's House delegation shrank from 7 to 6 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Kentucky kept 6 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Kentucky kept 6 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Kentucky kept 6 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"other","title":"Map enacted over governor's veto","text":"The General Assembly passed a new congressional map (SB 3) on January 8, 2022. Gov. Andy Beshear vetoed it on January 19, and the legislature overrode the veto the next day.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/kentucky/","https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/record/22rs/sb3.html"]},{"congress":118,"year":2023,"kind":"court","title":"Graham v. Adams","text":"A 2022 state-court suit claimed the congressional and state House maps were partisan gerrymanders that split counties unnecessarily. On December 14, 2023, the Kentucky Supreme Court upheld both maps, finding they did not clearly violate the state constitution.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/case/graham-v-adams/"],"mapChange":false}],"compactness":{"c002":{"KY-1":0.216,"KY-2":0.264},"c003":{"KY-1":0.216,"KY-2":0.264},"c004":{"KY-1":0.216,"KY-2":0.264},"c005":{"KY-1":0.216,"KY-2":0.264},"c006":{"KY-1":0.216,"KY-2":0.264},"c007":{"KY-1":0.209,"KY-2":0.238},"c008":{"KY-1":0.22,"KY-2":0.24,"KY-3":0.279,"KY-4":0.376,"KY-5":0.181,"KY-6":0.286},"c009":{"KY-1":0.22,"KY-2":0.24,"KY-3":0.279,"KY-4":0.376,"KY-5":0.181,"KY-6":0.286},"c010":{"KY-1":0.22,"KY-2":0.24,"KY-3":0.279,"KY-4":0.376,"KY-5":0.181,"KY-6":0.286},"c011":{"KY-1":0.22,"KY-2":0.24,"KY-3":0.279,"KY-4":0.376,"KY-5":0.181,"KY-6":0.286},"c012":{"KY-1":0.22,"KY-2":0.24,"KY-3":0.279,"KY-4":0.376,"KY-5":0.181,"KY-6":0.286},"c013":{"KY-1":0.257,"KY-2":0.327,"KY-3":0.262,"KY-4":0.232,"KY-5":0.236,"KY-6":0.355,"KY-7":0.15,"KY-8":0.434,"KY-9":0.323,"KY-10":0.275},"c014":{"KY-1":0.257,"KY-2":0.327,"KY-3":0.262,"KY-4":0.232,"KY-5":0.236,"KY-6":0.355,"KY-7":0.15,"KY-8":0.434,"KY-9":0.323,"KY-10":0.275},"c015":{"KY-1":0.257,"KY-2":0.327,"KY-3":0.262,"KY-4":0.232,"KY-5":0.236,"KY-6":0.355,"KY-7":0.15,"KY-8":0.434,"KY-9":0.323,"KY-10":0.275},"c016":{"KY-1":0.257,"KY-2":0.327,"KY-3":0.262,"KY-4":0.23